  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nAn anomalous upper level ridge continues to build into the region \nthis afternoon. H5 heights near 595 dam will translate to above \nnormal temperatures - about 5 degrees above normal per early \nSeptember standards. Surface high pressure will keep the area mostly \ndry, though  a few isolated showers cannot be ruled out in the high \nterrain. This pattern will continue into Wednesday. \n\nBy Thursday an upper level trough and associated jet is expected to \ndip into the Great Lakes region. An accompanying cold front will \nprovide an axis of forcing leading to a return of shower and storm \nchances by Thursday evening and night. Chances for precip will \ngenerally be greatest north of I-40 during this time. \n\nWhile the aforementioned jet will depart east, additional impulses \nand associated vorticity maxima will swing through the southern \nAppalachians Friday and Saturday, all while the frontal boundary \nlingers across the region. This will maintain daily chances for \nshowers and storms. Per latest RRFS and NAM12, may have to keep an \neye on Friday for a low potential of isolated strong storms. \nLatest soundings from these models suggest MLCAPE near 1500 J/kg, effective\nshear near 20kts, and mid-level lapse rates near 6.\n\nA transition to quasi-zonal flow is expected Sunday. While not \ntotally dry, a lesser coverage of precip chances is expected. This \nwill continue to be the case into the new work week as additional \nshortwave ridging builds atop the southern Appalachians.",
